-- title:
--   Ideals generated by idempotents lie in all powers
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring and let $S \subseteq R$ be a set of elements each of which is idempotent, i.e. $e \cdot e = e$ for every $e \in S$. Let $\mathfrak{p}$ be an ideal of $R$ containing $S$, and let $n$ be a natural number. Then the ideal $\mathrm{span}(S)$ generated by $S$ is contained in the $n$-th power $\mathfrak{p}^n$. Note that $\mathfrak{p}$ is an arbitrary ideal: no primality, properness or finiteness hypothesis is imposed, and $n$ is unconstrained, so the conclusion holds simultaneously for all $n$ (for $n = 0$ it is the trivial inclusion into $\mathfrak{p}^0 = R$). The set $S$ may be empty, in which case $\mathrm{span}(S)$ is the zero ideal.
--
--   An elementary closure statement: the ideal generated by idempotents lying in $\mathfrak{p}$ cannot be detected by the $\mathfrak{p}$-adic filtration, since each generator equals all of its own powers. It is used in the study of classifying maps on modular curves of full level, where kernels are exhibited as spans of idempotent elements and must be shown to lie in every power of a prime ideal.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Ideal_span_le_pow_of_forall_isIdempotentElem_of_subset.lean

